  "body": "Conjecture: the function open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object, whose documented rule says \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\", gives the same answer on two replies that the rule requires it to tell apart. Reply A is a code fence containing the JSON object {\"a\":1} followed by the words 'extra prose', and then the JSON object {\"b\":2} outside the fence. Reply B is just the JSON object {\"b\":2}. The rule requires recover_json_object(A) to return {\"a\":1}, because a fence holds an object, and recover_json_object(B) to return {\"b\":2}, because there is no fence and the last top-level object is {\"b\":2}. But the implementation's fenced pool contains the whole fence content '{\"a\":1} extra prose', which fails to parse, so it falls through to the top-level pool, where the last candidate is {\"b\":2}; thus it returns {\"b\":2} for both A and B. I would be wrong if the check returns {\"a\":1} for A and {\"b\":2} for B, or if the two answers differ in any way."
